WHAT THIS MEANS

Use these numbers. Copy them, republish them, chart them, quote them in a paper
or a blog post or a competitor's marketing, commercially or not. The only
condition is attribution.


HOW TO ATTRIBUTE

Credit "Spate Benchmark" and link to https://spate-benchmark.kainth.dev, note
that you changed the data if you did, and do not imply this project endorses
whatever you used it for.


PLEASE ALSO LINK BACK TO THE SPECIFIC RECORDS

Not a licence condition, and the request matters more here than the usual
courtesy. This benchmark is published by the author of one of the systems it
measures, so a reader who cannot get from a quoted number back to its provenance
has no way to check the one claim most worth checking.

Every record carries what it needs for that: the system version, the exact image
digest, `env_id` and `env_digest` for the hardware, `harness_version` and
`dataset_version` for the protocol and corpus, and a `run_id`. Quote a number
with enough of that to find it again.


A NUMBER ON ITS OWN CAN BE MISLEADING

Two records are only comparable when `harness_version`, `dataset_version` and
`env_id` all match — the site refuses to draw them on one axis otherwise, rather
than quietly averaging across them. Records also carry a `status`: one marked
`infra_bound` reached the shared infrastructure's limit rather than its own, and
is published as a measurement but not as a system comparison.

Current measurements come from a single macOS host and are labelled indicative
rather than authoritative. Carrying that caveat along is not required by the
licence. It is just accurate.
